## Verdict

Pure client-side SPA, single Vite entry, single origin. There is **zero** marketing/landing code in the repo today, and the router's design actively blocks a public `/` — the admin dashboard already owns `/`, and everything unmatched is swallowed by an auth-gated catch-all.

A second site is emittable from this repo, but not by adding a route — it needs either a Vite MPA entry or a separate build. Details below.

---

## 1. `/Users/ismailelqorchi/Desktop/iGrowFast/New Website/iGrowFast/vite.config.ts` — VERBATIM

```ts
import { defineConfig } from "vite";
import react from "@vitejs/plugin-react-swc";
import path from "path";

// https://vitejs.dev/config/
export default defineConfig(({ mode }) => ({
  server: {
    host: "::",
    port: 8080,
    hmr: {
      overlay: false,
    },
  },
  plugins: [react()],
  define: {
    global: "globalThis",
  },
  resolve: {
    alias: {
      "@": path.resolve(__dirname, "./src"),
      buffer: "buffer",
    },
  },
  optimizeDeps: {
    include: ["buffer"],
  },
  build: {
    // The status-icon picker (renderReactIcon) loads whole react-icons packs (~5.5 MB) and routes
    // are not yet lazy-split (~4.3 MB app chunk). Both are pre-existing; genuinely shrinking them is a
    // separate optimization (curated icon set / React.lazy routes). Raise the advisory threshold to
    // match today's reality rather than mask it with a risky reactive refactor.
    chunkSizeWarningLimit: 6000,
    rollupOptions: {
      output: {
        // Split heavy, dependency-leaf vendor libs into their own chunks. Shrinks the main
        // bundle under the 500 kB warning and gives react-icons its own chunk (resolving the
        // dynamic+static import warning). React/react-dom intentionally left to default chunking.
        manualChunks(id: string) {
          if (!id.includes("node_modules")) return;
          if (id.includes("react-icons")) return "react-icons";
          if (id.includes("@react-pdf")) return "react-pdf";
          if (id.includes("recharts") || id.includes("d3-")) return "charts";
          if (id.includes("framer-motion")) return "framer-motion";
          if (id.includes("node_modules/three") || id.includes("postprocessing")) return "three";
          if (id.includes("@supabase")) return "supabase";
          if (id.includes("xlsx")) return "xlsx";
        },
      },
    },
  },
}));
```

Reading the specifics asked for:
- **`root`** — NOT set. Defaults to cwd (repo root).
- **`base`** — NOT set. Defaults to `"/"`. A second site on a subpath would need this, or its own build.
- **`build.outDir`** — NOT set. Defaults to `dist/`.
- **`rollupOptions.input`** — **ABSENT**. This is the load-bearing fact: **single-entry**, implicitly `index.html` at repo root. There is **no multi-page setup**. Adding a second HTML entry means introducing `rollupOptions.input` as an object map — a config change that does not exist today.
- **`manualChunks`** — present, `output`-side only, vendor-splitting by `node_modules` path. It's entry-agnostic; a second entry would inherit the same vendor chunking for free.
- **`plugins`** — exactly one: `react()` from `@vitejs/plugin-react-swc`. No SSR plugin, no SSG plugin, no prerender plugin, no legacy plugin.
- Note `mode` is destructured in the config signature but **never used** in the returned object — dead param.

## 2. `package.json` scripts — VERBATIM

```json
"scripts": {
    "dev": "vite --mode development",
    "dev:dev": "vite --mode development",
    "dev:prod": "vite --mode production",
    "build": "cross-env NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=8192 vite build",
    "build:dev": "cross-env NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=8192 vite build --mode development",
    "lint": "eslint .",
    "preview": "vite preview",
    "test": "vitest run",
    "test:watch": "vitest"
}
```

**Exact build command: `cross-env NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=8192 vite build`.** Notable: there is **no `typecheck` script**. Typechecking is invoked directly as `npx tsc -b` in CI only (`.github/workflows/deploy-frontend.yml:37`), with an explicit comment that vite/esbuild transpiles without typechecking and a broken TS file once shipped a runtime ReferenceError. The 8 GB heap bump is a signal of how large the current single bundle already is.

## 3. `index.html` — the single SPA entry

`/Users/ismailelqorchi/Desktop/iGrowFast/New Website/iGrowFast/index.html`, 65 lines, `<html lang="fr">`. Body is just `<div id="root"></div>` + `<script type="module" src="/src/main.tsx">`.

**Sharp finding:** the meta in this file is *already written as marketing copy for a site that doesn't exist*. Title `iGrowFast — Pilotez votre e-commerce sans dix onglets`, full OG + Twitter card set, `og:image` → `https://igrowfast.com/og-image.png`, and `<link rel="canonical" href="https://igrowfast.com/" />` (index.html:11). But this app deploys to **`app.igrowfast.com`** (deploy workflow:61), not `igrowfast.com`. So today every authenticated app route self-canonicalizes to an apex domain that serves nothing from this repo. That meta block is the marketing site's meta, sitting on the SPA shell.

## 4. tsconfig — project references

- `tsconfig.json`: `"files": []`, `"references": [{"path": "./tsconfig.app.json"}, {"path": "./tsconfig.node.json"}]`. This is what makes `tsc -b` real.
- `tsconfig.app.json`: **`"include": ["src"]`** — the whole subtree, unfiltered.
- `tsconfig.node.json`: `"include": ["vite.config.ts"]`, `strict: true`.

**Answer to the question asked: YES — a new `src/` subtree (e.g. `src/marketing/`) is automatically typechecked by `npx tsc -b`, with no config change.** `include: ["src"]` is a directory glob, so a new folder is picked up for free, and the CI gate at workflow line 37 covers it on day one.

Caveat worth knowing: `tsconfig.app.json` sets `strict: false`, `noImplicitAny: false`, `noUnusedLocals: false`, `noUnusedParameters: false`, `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch: false`; `tsconfig.json` additionally sets `strictNullChecks: false`. A new marketing subtree inherits that loose config. Getting strict typing on new code would require its own tsconfig project, not just a folder.

## 5. `src/` structure + router

Top level: `App.tsx`, `main.tsx`, `index.css`, `vite-env.d.ts`, and dirs `components/ data/ hooks/ lib/ pages/ whatsapp/`. `pages/` = `admin/ analytics/ courier/ mediabuyer/ owner/ seller/` + `Auth.tsx Register.tsx ChangeCredentials.tsx AdsHistory.tsx NotFound.tsx`.

- **Router library:** `react-router-dom` `^6.30.1`.
- **Router entry:** `src/App.tsx` — all routes declared inline in one `<Routes>` block (App.tsx:210-367). No separate router file, no data-router / `createBrowserRouter`.
- **`BrowserRouter`?** Yes — App.tsx:209. Not HashRouter, not a data router. `main.tsx` is a bare `createRoot(...).render(<App />)`.
- **Catch-all `"*"`?** Yes, and this is the critical structural fact. **App.tsx:304**: `<Route path="*" element={<ProtectedRoute allowedRoles={['super admin', 'admin']}><BusinessGuard><AppLayout><Routes>…</Routes></AppLayout></BusinessGuard></ProtectedRoute>} />`. The admin app is not mounted at `/admin/*` — it *is* the catch-all, and its inner router declares `<Route path="/" element={<Dashboard />} />` (App.tsx:309). **The admin dashboard owns `/`.**
- **Role-based paths:** explicit prefixes `/owner/*` (:217), `/mediabuyer/*` (:240), `/courier/*` (:265), `/seller/*` (:280), each wrapped in `<ProtectedRoute allowedRoles={[…]}>` + a role layout + a nested `<Routes>`; admin = the `"*"` fallback. Public routes today are exactly three: `/auth` (:211), `/register/:token` (:213), `/account/change/:token` (:215).
- **Lazy bundles:** only the WhatsApp module is code-split — 6 `React.lazy(() => import("@/whatsapp/…"))` at App.tsx:100-109, each wrapped in `<Suspense>`. **Every other page is a static top-level import** (App.tsx:6-95, ~85 eager imports). The vite.config comment confirms: "routes are not yet lazy-split (~4.3 MB app chunk)".

**Does an unauthenticated visitor hitting `/` get redirected to login? YES.** Exact mechanism, two hops:
1. `/` matches the `"*"` route (App.tsx:304) → renders `<ProtectedRoute allowedRoles={['super admin','admin']}>`.
2. `/Users/ismailelqorchi/Desktop/iGrowFast/New Website/iGrowFast/src/components/auth/ProtectedRoute.tsx:17-25` — the exact code:
```ts
useEffect(() => {
    const checkAuth = async () => {
        const { data: { session } } = await supabase.auth.getSession();

        if (!session) {
            navigate('/auth');
            setLoading(false);
            return;
        }
```
While `loading` it renders `<FullscreenLoader sublabel="Nous vérifions vos autorisations." />` (:85-87), and returns `null` when unauthenticated (:94). So `/` is a client-side loader flash → `/auth`. Also `navigate('/auth')` on `SIGNED_OUT` (:74-76) and on missing/inactive profile (:37, :46).

**Collision to be explicit about:** in React Router v6 route ranking is by specificity, not declaration order — a static `path="/"` outranks the `path="*"` splat. So adding a top-level `<Route path="/" element={<Landing/>}/>` to this same `<Routes>` **would take `/` away from the admin Dashboard** (App.tsx:309) rather than coexist with it. A marketing `/` inside this router is not additive; it displaces. This is the single biggest reason a second site wants a second entry/build rather than a new route.

## 6. Existing marketing/landing code: NONE

Grepped `landing|marketing|homepage|public-site` across `src/`, `index.html`, `public/`. Nine files hit, **all false positives** — every one is ad-platform vocabulary, not a marketing site:
- `src/lib/campaignConstants.tsx:106,109` — `ConversionType = 'landing_page' | …`, label `'Landing page'`, `contentLabel: 'Landing URL'` (the *advertiser's* landing page URL field).
- `src/components/meta/shared/constants.ts:47` — Meta objective `'LANDING_PAGE_VIEWS'`.
- `src/pages/owner/Plans.tsx:528,619` — UI copy "Configure pricing, billing cycle and marketing features", placeholder "Short marketing description…".
- `src/pages/admin/ExpensesTypes.tsx:338,377,389` — "Marketing" as an *expense bucket* name example.
- Remainder (`AdsHistory.tsx`, `CampaignForm.tsx`, `Campaigns.tsx`, `AdStep.tsx`, `whatsapp-base.css`) — same class.

**`public/` contents:** `.htaccess`, `announcement-deck.html` (388 KB), `Background.webm` (4.4 MB), `BG.svg` (209 KB), `og-image.png` (464 KB), `manifest.json`, `sw.js`, `fonts/`, `Logo/`, `providers/`.

Two things in `public/` matter:
- **`announcement-deck.html` is a real precedent** — a standalone static HTML page already ships alongside the SPA and lands at `dist/announcement-deck.html` (verified in `dist/`). But it is a **`public/` copy-as-is asset, not a Vite entry**: no bundling, no transform, no asset hashing, no shared chunks. A marketing site built that way gets none of the React/Tailwind/vendor-chunk machinery. It's precedent for *a second HTML file existing*, not for *a second built site*.
- **`public/.htaccess` is Apache SPA-rewrite config** (`RewriteRule . /index.html [L]`) — but the origin is **nginx** on the toolbox VPS (deploy workflow comment, lines 1-9). This file appears vestigial/dead at the current origin. Flagging it because the SPA-fallback rewrite is exactly the rule a second site would have to negotiate with, and the real one is not in this repo.

## 7. SEO / SSR / prerender — pure client-side SPA, stated honestly

**There is no SSR, no SSG, no prerender, no hydration. None.** Grepped `ssr|prerender|vite-ssg|vite-plugin-ssr|react-helmet|react-snap|renderToString` across `package.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `src/main.tsx` — **zero hits**. Dependencies confirm: no Next, no Remix, no Astro, no helmet, no head-management library of any kind.

Consequences, plainly:
- Meta tags are **static in `index.html` only** — one fixed title/description/OG set served identically for every URL. Nothing can vary per-route, because nothing renders server-side and nothing manages `<head>` client-side.
- **No `robots.txt`, no `sitemap.xml`** anywhere in the repo (searched to depth 2, excluding `node_modules`/`.git`).
- First paint requires executing a ~4.3 MB JS bundle; `/` then does an async `getSession()` before it renders anything at all.
- `dist/` contains exactly two HTML files: `index.html` (built) and `announcement-deck.html` (copied).

For an SEO-bearing marketing site, this stack gives you nothing today — a marketing site emitted from this repo either accepts client-only rendering with one static meta block, or introduces prerendering that currently has no foothold in the config.

## Deploy pipeline (bears directly on "a second site")

`.github/workflows/deploy-frontend.yml`: push to `main` → `npm ci` → **`npx tsc -b` (gate)** → `npm run build` → rsync **`dist/`** → `igf-deploy@37.60.250.211:/opt/igf-frontend/www/releases/<ts>-<sha>/` → symlink flip `www/current` → prune to last 5 → `curl -sfI https://app.igrowfast.com`.

The pipeline ships **one `dist/` to one origin serving one hostname**. A second site on a second hostname (e.g. apex `igrowfast.com`) needs an nginx server block that does not exist in this repo — `ops/` has backup/agent/systemd/whatsapp material but **no nginx or `.conf` file at all** (searched). That config lives only on the box. Whoever plans this must not assume the origin is repo-controlled; it isn't.

options.mechanism:
BUILD. vite.config.ts:33-49 currently has `rollupOptions` containing ONLY an `output` key (manualChunks); there is no `input` key — the build is implicitly single-entry on `index.html` at repo root (`root` unset → cwd; `base` unset → "/"; `build.outDir` unset → dist/). Add a sibling `input`:

    rollupOptions: {
      input: {
        main: path.resolve(__dirname, "index.html"),
        site: path.resolve(__dirname, "site/index.html"),
      },
      output: { manualChunks(id) { /* unchanged */ } },
    }

Emission rule that governs everything downstream: Vite writes each HTML entry to a path MIRRORING its location relative to `root`. `index.html` → `dist/index.html`. `site/index.html` → `dist/site/index.html`. You CANNOT make the marketing entry emit to `dist/index.html` — that slot is the app's — without a plugin or a post-build move. So the marketing HTML permanently lives at the `/site/` prefix inside dist, and nginx does the mapping from apex-`/` to `/site/index.html`. That is the whole trick.

New files (only three):
- `site/index.html` — hand-written shell, `<div id="root"></div>` + `<script type="module" src="/src/marketing/main.tsx">`, with its OWN static `<head>`: title/description/canonical `https://igrowfast.com/`/og:url/og:image. Deliberately does NOT link `/manifest.json` (else the apex becomes installable as the app PWA) and does NOT copy the PWA/apple-mobile-web-app meta from index.html:38-53.
- `src/marketing/main.tsx` — bare `createRoot(...).render(<Landing />)`. Must NOT copy main.tsx's Buffer polyfill (that exists only for @react-pdf/renderer, src/main.tsx:7-9).
- `src/marketing/Landing.tsx` + `src/marketing/marketing.css`.

Placing the TS under `src/` is load-bearing: tsconfig.app.json:30 is `"include": ["src"]` — an unfiltered directory glob — so `src/marketing/**` is covered by the CI gate `npx tsc -b` (deploy-frontend.yml:37) with zero config change. `site/index.html` itself isn't typechecked, but it's HTML.

CHUNKING — does marketing avoid supabase-js? Rollup does entry-point code splitting: each HTML entry gets its own entry chunk; modules reachable from ONE entry live in that entry's chunk; modules reachable from BOTH get hoisted to a shared chunk. `manualChunks` (vite.config.ts:38-47) is an `output`-side function and is entry-agnostic — it only decides WHICH chunk a node_modules module lands in, not whether an entry loads it. So:
- If `src/marketing/main.tsx` imports only react-dom/client + Landing + marketing.css, and Landing imports nothing touching `@/lib/supabase` or `@/App`, then `@supabase/supabase-js` is NOT in the marketing module graph, the `supabase` manualChunk is never reachable from it, and `dist/site/index.html` will carry NO `<link rel="modulepreload" href="/assets/supabase-*.js">`. Contrast the app: dist/index.html today emits exactly 6 modulepreloads (react-icons, framer-motion, supabase, react-pdf, charts, xlsx) — those are the app's reachable manualChunks, and the marketing entry inherits none of them by default.
- ACCEPTANCE TEST (this is the entire safety story): after build, `grep -c "supabase\|react-icons\|xlsx\|react-pdf\|charts" dist/site/index.html` must be 0.
- Realistic marketing payload: react + react-dom is ~45 KB gzip floor, plus your Landing code. If Landing uses framer-motion, the existing `framer-motion` chunk (127,310 raw / 41,910 gzip) becomes shared between both entries — that's fine and is a benefit. Order of magnitude: 60–120 KB gzip vs. the app's measured 3,026,703 bytes gzip across its 7 eager chunks. Two orders of magnitude better.

TAILWIND — a real, unavoidable coupling. tailwind.config.ts:6 is `content: ["./pages/**/*.{ts,tsx}", "./components/**/*.{ts,tsx}", "./app/**/*.{ts,tsx}", "./src/**/*.{ts,tsx}"]`. A single Vite build runs a single PostCSS pipeline with ONE Tailwind config, therefore ONE generated utility surface. `src/marketing/**` is inside `./src/**` so it's scanned — good — but the utilities emitted into the marketing entry's CSS are the union across ALL of src/. dist/assets/index-BECRfdx7.css is 175,330 raw / 27,309 gzip. The marketing page will carry roughly that whole utility set even importing its own `marketing.css`. 27 KB gzip is small enough to accept, but be honest: you do not get a scoped CSS surface out of this option. Getting one requires a second Tailwind config + per-file postcss override, which is a fight.

NGINX — two server blocks, one dist. Existing frontend origin (inferred from deploy-frontend.yml:3-9, 50-56) roots at `/opt/igf-frontend/www/current` (a relative symlink → `releases/<ts>-<sha>`, flipped by `ln -sfn`, picked up without restart).

    # existing block, unchanged except two additions
    server {
      server_name app.igrowfast.com;
      root /opt/igf-frontend/www/current;
      location ^~ /site/ { return 404; }          # ADD: kill the duplicate-content leak
      location / { try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; }   # SPA fallback (verified live: unknown paths → 200 + index.html)
    }

    # NEW block
    server {
      server_name igrowfast.com;
      root /opt/igf-frontend/www/current;
      location = /            { try_files /site/index.html =404; }
      location = /index.html  { return 301 https://app.igrowfast.com/; }   # else apex serves the APP shell
      location ^~ /assets/    { try_files $uri =404; add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"; }
      location /              { try_files $uri /site/index.html; }
    }

Three sharp details: (1) `location = /` is required — a plain `try_files $uri $uri/` at `/` hits the directory, `index index.html` fires, and the APEX SERVES THE APP. (2) `/index.html` on the apex is a real leak and needs the explicit redirect. (3) `/site/index.html` on app.igrowfast.com is a real duplicate-content leak and needs the explicit 404. Both hostnames share `/assets/`, `/Logo/`, `/og-image.png`, `/manifest.json`, `/sw.js` off the same root — which is exactly why `base: "/"` needs no change.

I DO NOT KNOW the actual nginx config. Recon is explicit: "No nginx config and no frontend cloudflared config exist anywhere in the repo — that infrastructure is unversioned and lives only on the toolbox box." Unknown: whether nginx runs on the host or in Docker (the workflow comment "isolated nginx+cloudflared stack" + "cannot touch docker or the tunnel" strongly implies containerized), the config file path, the compose file path, the container name, the listen port, and whether the existing block already sets `index`. These must be read on the box before writing anything. Do not assume the block above matches what's there.

CLOUDFLARED / CLOUDFLARE. Today the apex is an edge-generated 302 to https://app.igrowfast.com/ with path+query discarded (verified: /pricing, /robots.txt, /foo/bar?x=1, /index.html all → `location: https://app.igrowfast.com/`). The response carries `server: cloudflare` + `cf-ray` but NO `cf-cache-status`, NO `alt-svc`, and NO origin headers — proving it terminates at the edge rules engine and never reaches an origin. Four steps:
1. DELETE the apex redirect rule. It is either Rules → Redirect Rules (Single Redirect) or the legacy Rules → Page Rules "Forwarding URL" — externally indistinguishable, must be identified in the dashboard.
2. Apex DNS: replace the proxied A records (172.67.190.28, 104.21.33.135) and AAAA (2606:4700:3036::ac43:be1c, 2606:4700:3033::6815:2187) with a PROXIED CNAME to `<frontend-tunnel-uuid>.cfargotunnel.com`. CF's apex CNAME flattening handles the apex case.
3. cloudflared ingress on the toolbox — add `- hostname: igrowfast.com` pointing at the SAME service value the existing `app.igrowfast.com` rule uses, above the catch-all `- service: http_status:404`.
4. Reload cloudflared.
I DO NOT KNOW the frontend tunnel's UUID, its config path, or its current ingress service URL. The only tunnel in the repo is `ops/systemd/box/cloudflared-backup.service` (tunnel 5edf36b8) which serves backup-api.igrowfast.com from the .102 box and is unrelated. These must be read on .211.
TLS needs nothing: the live cert SAN is `DNS:igrowfast.com, DNS:*.igrowfast.com` (Google Trust Services via CF Universal SSL, valid to Sep 28 2026) — apex already covered.
EMAIL LANDMINE: the apex carries `MX 5 mx1.hostinger.com / 10 mx2.hostinger.com`, `TXT "v=spf1 include:_spf.mail.hostinger.com ~all"`, and `_dmarc "v=DMARC1; p=reject"`, with no DKIM at the standard Hostinger selector. The A/AAAA records are independent of MX. Touch ONLY the A/AAAA→CNAME swap. Under `p=reject` with alignment resting on SPF alone, breaking these bounces real mail.

CI. Zero workflow changes. deploy-frontend.yml:50-52 rsyncs `dist/` with `-az --delete` — a dist containing a new `site/` directory ships automatically. The symlink flip and the last-5 retention are unaffected. Optionally append a second verify line next to the existing `curl -sfI https://app.igrowfast.com` (deploy-frontend.yml:61).

